L000168	
L000169	The Coulomb potential in terms of the scaling factors $(s_{ik})$ is expressed as
L000170	\begin{equation}
L000171		V_C(\rho, \theta) = \sum_{i<k} \frac{Z_{i} Z_{k} e^{2}}{r_{i k}(\theta)} = \frac{1}{\rho} \sum_{i<k} \frac{Z_{i} Z_{k} e^{2}}{s_{i k}(\theta)}.
L000172	\end{equation}
L000173	The nuclear potential is taken in the classical Woods-Saxon (WS) form,
L000174	\begin{equation}
L000175			V_N(\rho, \theta) = \sum_{i<k} \, \frac{V_{ik}}{1 + \exp\big[(\rho s_{ik}(\theta) - R_{ik}) / a_{ik}\big]}.
L000176		\label{V_nrho}
L000177	\end{equation}
L000178	Here, $\rho s_{ij}(\theta)$ (or $\rho s_{jk}(\theta)$) denotes the distance between a given $\alpha$ particle and the core of the parent nucleus, and $\rho s_{ik}(\theta)$ corresponds to the distance between the two $\alpha$ particles. The parameters $V$, $R$, and $a$ with subscript $ik$ correspond to the $\alpha$–$\alpha$ interaction potential, and those with subscript $ij$ (or $jk$) refer to the $\alpha$–core interaction potential.
L000179	
L000180	Using the above hyper-radial potential, the radial wave function can be obtained by solving the corresponding hyperradial Schrödinger equation, which is given by
L000181	\begin{equation}
L000182	\left[ -\frac{\hbar^2}{2\mu_\rho} \frac{d^2}{d\rho^2} + V(\rho, \theta) \right] \varphi_{nlj}(\rho; \theta) = Q_{\alpha\alpha} \varphi_{nlj}(\rho; \theta),
L000183	\end{equation}
L000184	where $\mu_\rho$ equals $m \times amu$ (or $m \times u$) and $Q_{\alpha\alpha}$ is the double-$\alpha$ decay energy associated with ground-state to ground-state transitions. The quasibound solution $\varphi_{nlj}(\rho; \theta)$ is obtained by imposing the outgoing Coulomb-wave boundary condition
L000185	\begin{equation}
L000186	\varphi_{nlj}(\rho; \theta) = C(\theta) \left[ F_l(\eta_\rho, k_\rho \rho) + i G_l(\eta_\rho, k_\rho \rho) \right],
L000187	\end{equation}
L000188	where wave number $k_\rho = \sqrt{2\mu_\rho Q_{\alpha\alpha} / (\hbar^2)}$ and $C(\theta)$ is the normalization constant. For a certain orientation angle $\theta$ between 2$\alpha$ particles, the corresponding decay width can then be expressed as
L000189	\begin{equation}
L000190		\Gamma_{\alpha\alpha}(\theta) = \frac{\hbar^2 k_\rho}{\mu_\rho} \frac{\left| \varphi_{nlj}(\rho_{\text{m}}; \theta) \right|^2}{F_l(\eta_\rho, k_\rho \rho_{\text{m}})^2 + G_l(\eta_\rho, k_\rho \rho_{\text{m}})^2},
L000191	\end{equation}
L000192	where $\rho_m$ denotes a matching radius in the asymptotic Coulomb region, taken sufficiently large to lie beyond the range of the nuclear interaction. By averaging of $\Gamma_{\alpha\alpha}(\theta)$ in all directions, the penetrability is obtained as
L000193	\begin{equation}
L000194		{\Gamma_{\alpha\alpha}}=\int d\theta\,w(\theta)\,\Gamma_{\alpha\alpha}(\theta),
L000195		\qquad
L000196		\int d\theta w(\theta)=1.
L000197		\label{Pavg}
L000198	\end{equation}
L000199	The angular probability distribution $w(\theta)$, derived from the angular part of the total wavefunction for the present three-body system, is approximated as:
L000200	\begin{equation}
L000201		w(\theta) = \left| \mathcal{N} \exp\left(-\frac{(\theta - \pi)^2}{4\sigma^2}\right) \right|^2,
L000202	\end{equation}
L000203	which favors the back-to-back configuration ($\theta = \pi$). This result is consistent with both the geometric angular factors and the tunneling dynamics established in Refs.~\cite{PRL_2a, Zhao, Denisov}. Further details regarding the derivation are provided in the Supplementary Material.
L000204	
L000205	In an analogous manner, the $\Gamma_\alpha$ of the single-$\alpha$ decay width is obtained from the same Coulomb-matching expression as in the double-$\alpha$ case, with $\varphi_{nlj}(\rho_m; \theta)$ replaced by $\varphi_{nlj}(R)$ and $(\mu_\rho, k_\rho)$ replaced by $(\mu, k)$, where $k$ is defined in terms of the single-$\alpha$ decay energy $Q_\alpha$. In this process, the interaction potential $V(r)$ consists of the nuclear potential $V_N(r) = V / \bigl[1 + \exp((r - R^{\prime}) / a)\bigr]$, the Coulomb potential $V_C(r)$, and the centrifugal potential~\cite{XuChang}. The nuclear radius parameter $R^{\prime}$ in the sequential decay scenario is determined separately by the Wildermuth condition~\cite{XuChang, wildermuth}, where $G = 2n + \ell = \sum_{i=1}^{4} g_i$. In this context, the nucleus remaining after the simultaneous emission of two $\alpha$ particles is equivalent to that obtained after the emission of $\alpha_2$ from the intermediate nucleus in the sequential-decay mode (see the right panel of Fig.~\ref{alpha_decay}). Accordingly, the radius parameter $R_{ij}$ (or $R_{jk}$) in Eq.~(\ref{V_nrho}) is identified with $R'$, representing the nuclear radius in the sequential-decay picture at the emission of the second $\alpha$ particle.
L000206	
L000207	A direct comparison between the decay widths of double-$\alpha$ and single-$\alpha$ decay offers not only a quantitative measure of the relative likelihood of this exotic mode but also a practical criterion for identifying the most promising nuclei for experimental searches, thereby motivating the definition of the branching ratio (BR) as
L000208	\begin{equation}
L000209		\mathrm{BR} = \text{log}_{10}(\frac{\Gamma_{\alpha\alpha}}{\Gamma_\alpha}).
L000210	\end{equation}
L000211	The half-life of double-$\alpha$ decay is defined as
L000212	\begin{equation}
L000213		T_{\alpha\alpha} = \frac{\hbar \ln 2}{P_{0}\Gamma_{\alpha\alpha}},
L000214	\end{equation}
L000215	where $P_0$ is the preformation factor, which is evaluated using the cluster formation model (CFM)~\cite{Deng_2015, PhysRevC.93.044326}.
